This file's presence, not its contents, is the whole message: s6-supervise
starts a service whose directory contains a file called `down` in the DOWN
state instead of starting it. s6 never opens the file, so the reasoning can
live in it rather than in a comment somewhere else that would drift away from
it. (case-moonraker.sh checks that too, with this text in place, because a
fact this file depends on is worth measuring rather than believing.)

WHY MOONRAKER SHIPS DOWN. The gate on the web stack is MOD_WEB in anvil.conf,
and it is ONE switch across two services -- init.d/S60nginx and
init.d/S62moonraker -- because "no web stack" is one intention and there is no
useful printer with Mainsail and no moonraker behind it. That switch is read
AT RUNTIME, on the printer, out of a file the user edits over ssh; this payload
is built ONCE, weeks earlier, by bin/patch.sh. So the scandir cannot be where
the decision is made: a service directory that ships without this file is a
service the scanner starts a fraction of a second after init.d/S40s6 runs,
before anything has read anvil.conf and regardless of what it says. MOD_WEB=0
would mean "moonraker runs for two seconds on every boot and is then shot",
which is not what it says on the tin and is worse than not having the switch.

So the shipped state is "down", meaning: THE SCANNER DOES NOT DECIDE WHEN
MOONRAKER STARTS. init.d/S62moonraker does, at the point in the boot where
anvil.conf has been sourced, by calling svc_s6_up -- which overrides this file
for as long as that s6-supervise lives. MOD_WEB=1 and it comes up there;
MOD_WEB=0 and it never comes up at all, which is exactly what the switch has
always promised. The same is then true of every other way in: `S62moonraker
start` over ssh re-reads anvil.conf, and a user who sets MOD_WEB=0 and reboots
gets no API server rather than one that has to be caught and stopped.

IT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN ANYWHERE ELSE, and that is worth saying plainly.
Moonraker is not a service that idles when it is started too early: it opens
its database, binds :7125, and connects to klippy's socket at /tmp/uds. A
scanner-started moonraker would be doing all of that before S60nginx, before
S70klipper and before anything had decided whether klippy is even meant to
run -- and the copy that init.d/S62moonraker started afterwards would then be
the SECOND one, fighting the first for the port, in the exact respawn loop the
sweep at the top of the `run` script exists to prevent. The `down` file is what
keeps that loop hypothetical.

THE COST, stated honestly, because it is the one thing this arrangement gives
up. `down` is consulted when s6-supervise STARTS, not when the service dies:
once S62moonraker has brought moonraker up, s6 keeps restarting it on every
crash for as long as that supervisor lives, which is the entire point of the
migration and is unaffected by this file. What it does mean is that if
s6-supervise ITSELF is killed -- not moonraker, the supervisor -- s6-svscan
starts a fresh one, that fresh one reads this file, and moonraker stays down
until something runs `S62moonraker start` again. That is a rare failure
(s6-supervise is a small C program that blocks in poll() and is not the thing
that crashes) with an honest signal (`S62moonraker status` says it is not
running, and says so from s6-svstat rather than from a pidfile) and a one-line
fix from an ssh session. The alternative -- deleting this file at runtime once
MOD_WEB has been read -- buys back that corner case at the price of a payload
that rewrites its own install tree, which every path in this mod is arranged
not to do.

This file is not a record of anything having gone wrong: it is the state
moonraker ships in, on every build, and nothing removes it.
